\section{Getting started}{

A simulation always follows the same three steps.
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Choose the inspection times} \code{t}. Three forms are accepted by every
\code{sim_} function:
\itemize{
\item a vector, for instance \code{seq(0, 10, by = 0.5)}: all the trajectories
share these times, and their number is given by \code{n_traj};
\item a list of vectors, one per trajectory, for instance
\code{list(c(0, 1, 3), c(0, 2, 4, 6))};
\item the result of \code{\link[=inspection_times]{inspection_times()}}, for instance with the pattern
\code{"increasing"} for trajectories inspected more and more often.
}

The times of each trajectory start at 0 and are strictly increasing.
\item \strong{Simulate} with the function of the chosen process and its
parameters, for instance \code{\link[=sim_gamma]{sim_gamma()}} for a gamma process.
For a non-homogeneous process, give the function of time, for instance
\code{A = function(t) t^1.5}.
\item \strong{Store and use the result} \code{x}. Its form depends on the times:
\itemize{
\item same times for all the trajectories: a matrix with the column \code{t}
and one column per trajectory, \code{X1}, ..., \code{Xn}; the \eqn{k}-th
trajectory is \code{x[, "Xk"]};
\item different times: a list with one data frame (\code{t}, \code{X}) per
trajectory; the \eqn{k}-th trajectory is \code{x[[k]]}.
}

In both cases, \code{x} prints the model, the parameters and the first
simulated values, \code{traj_df(x)} gives all the data in one data frame
(\code{id}, \code{t}, \code{X}, and \code{dt}, \code{dX} with \code{increments = TRUE}) and
\code{plot_traj(x)} plots the trajectories.
}

Use \code{set.seed()} before a simulation to make it reproducible.
}

\section{Exact simulation}{

For the first five processes, which have independent increments, the joint
distribution at the inspection times is determined by the distributions of
the increments: the simulation draws independent increments with the
prescribed distributions and cumulates them, without any time
discretization. The inverse gamma process, whose increments are dependent,
uses a specific exact algorithm.
}

\examples{
# 1. Inspection times: 10 trajectories, inspected more and more often
plan <- inspection_times("increasing", n = 20, n_traj = 10, tmax = 100)
plot_plan(plan)

# 2. Simulation of a homogeneous gamma process
set.seed(1)
x <- sim_gamma(plan, a = 2, b = 3)

# 3. Use of the result: the same times for all the trajectories give a
#    matrix (column t, then X1, ..., X10)
x
x[, "X1"]
head(traj_df(x, increments = TRUE))
plot_traj(x)
}
